# CommonJS Modules

The [`import` and `export` syntax](<12 - Modules Overview.webnb>) from Lesson 3 is the modern standard, and it is called **ES modules**. Node.js existed before that syntax did, and it needed a way to split code across files, so it used an older system called **CommonJS**.

CommonJS has not gone anywhere. You will meet it in Node code, in configuration files, and in plenty of published packages, and you will meet it in this course: the servers you write in Module 2 and Module 3 use it, and so does the Webpack configuration file in the next lesson. Both systems do the same job with different words.

## Requiring

Where ES modules say `import`, CommonJS calls a function named **`require`**. It returns whatever the other file exports.

There are three kinds of thing you can require, and the shape of the argument tells you which:

```javascript
const http = require('http');              // a module built into Node
const express = require('express');        // a package installed with npm
const utils = require('./math-utils.js');  // a file of your own
```

A bare name means one of Node's own modules or an installed package; a name starting with `./` or `../` means a path to your own file, exactly as in an ES module import.

Requiring works in these notebooks for a few modules, `path` among them, so this runs:

```{node autorun}
const path = require('path');

console.log(path.basename('/workspace/src/app.js'));
console.log(path.join('src', 'app.js'));
```

### Taking Pieces Out with Braces

Often you want two or three things from a module rather than the whole object, and you will see that written like this:

```{node autorun}
const { basename, join } = require('path');

console.log(basename('/workspace/src/app.js'));
console.log(join('src', 'app.js'));
```

The braces are **destructuring**: they pull named properties out of the object on the right and declare a variable for each one. That line is a shorter way of writing:

```javascript
const path = require('path');
const basename = path.basename;
const join = path.join;
```

It reads much like a named import, and it is worth recognizing, because real Node code is full of it:

```javascript
const { WebSocketServer } = require('ws');
```

## Exporting

Where ES modules use the `export` keyword, a CommonJS file has an object called **`module.exports`**, and whatever you put on it is what `require` hands back. There are two ways to write it.

Replace the whole object:

```{node autorun}
// math-utils.js

function double(number) {
  return number * 2;
}

function triple(number) {
  return number * 3;
}

module.exports = {
  double: double,
  triple: triple
};

console.log(module.exports.double(5));
```

Or add one property at a time:

```{node autorun}
// math-utils.js

exports.double = function (number) {
  return number * 2;
};

exports.triple = function (number) {
  return number * 3;
};

console.log(exports.triple(5));
```

`exports` starts out pointing at the same object as `module.exports`, which is why adding to it works. The trap is mixing the two styles: if you assign `module.exports = { ... }` *after* adding things to `exports`, you have replaced the object and the earlier additions are gone.

The requiring file then gets exactly that object:

```javascript
const utils = require('./math-utils.js');

console.log(utils.double(5));
```

## The Two Systems Side by Side

| Job | ES modules | CommonJS |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bring in named things | `import { join } from 'path';` | `const { join } = require('path');` |
| Bring in everything | `import * as path from 'path';` | `const path = require('path');` |
| Bring in the main thing | `import path from 'path';` | `const path = require('path');` |
| Share one value | `export default thing;` | `module.exports = thing;` |
| Share several values | `export { a, b };` | `module.exports = { a, b };` |
| Turn it on | `type="module"` in the HTML | nothing; it is Node's default |

Two differences go beyond spelling.

**`require` is an ordinary function call.** It can sit inside an `if`, or halfway down a file, and it runs at that moment. `import` statements are declarations, processed before any of the file's own code runs, which is what lets tools work out a project's whole dependency graph without executing it.

**What comes back is a plain object.** An ES module import is a [read-only live binding](<14 - Importing Variables.webnb#imported-values-are-read-only>), as we saw in Lesson 3. A `require` result is just a value you are holding, so nothing stops you reassigning your own variable.

## Which Should You Write?

For new code, prefer ES modules. For code in this course, follow whatever the file you are editing already uses, and read the surrounding lines before adding an import to a server file.

Node decides which system a `.js` file uses from the nearest `package.json`. With no `"type"` field it treats the file as CommonJS; with `"type": "module"` it treats it as an ES module. Two extensions settle the question outright, `.cjs` for CommonJS and `.mjs` for ES modules, which helps when one project needs both.

## Exercises

1. Export both functions so that `require` hands back an object with `celsius` and `fahrenheit` on it.

    ```{node}
    // temps.js

    function celsius(tempF) {
      return (tempF - 32) * 5 / 9;
    }

    function fahrenheit(tempC) {
      return tempC * 9 / 5 + 32;
    }

    // Write your export here
    ```
    ```+solution
    // temps.js

    function celsius(tempF) {
      return (tempF - 32) * 5 / 9;
    }

    function fahrenheit(tempC) {
      return tempC * 9 / 5 + 32;
    }

    module.exports = {
      celsius: celsius,
      fahrenheit: fahrenheit
    };
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(typeof module.exports.celsius).should('equal', 'function').run('Put `celsius` on `module.exports`', 'Exported celsius');
    wrap(typeof module.exports.fahrenheit).should('equal', 'function').run('Put `fahrenheit` on `module.exports` as well', 'Exported fahrenheit');
    wrap(module.exports.fahrenheit(100)).should('equal', 212).run('The exported `fahrenheit` should still convert 100C to 212F', 'The exported functions work');
    ```

2. Require just `extname` and `join` from `path`, using the braces form, and use both.

    ```{node}
    // Write your require statement here

    const fullPath = /* join 'src' and 'index.js' */;
    const extension = /* the extension of fullPath */;

    console.log(fullPath, extension);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const { extname, join } = require('path');

    const fullPath = join('src', 'index.js');
    const extension = extname(fullPath);

    console.log(fullPath, extension);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(/const\s*\{[^}]*\}\s*=\s*require\(/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Use the destructuring form, `const { ... } = require(\'path\');`', 'Used the braces form');
    wrap(fullPath).should('equal', 'src/index.js').run('`join(\'src\', \'index.js\')` should give `src/index.js`', 'Joined the path');
    wrap(extension).should('equal', '.js').run('`extname(fullPath)` should give `.js`', 'Read the extension');
    ```

3. A file contains `export default connectToDatabase;`. What is the CommonJS equivalent?

    ```{mcq}
    Rewrite that line for a CommonJS file.
    correct: Correct! Assigning to `module.exports` replaces the whole exported value, which is what a default export does.
    wrong: Check the comparison table for the row about sharing exactly one value.
    [x] `module.exports = connectToDatabase;`
    [ ] `exports default connectToDatabase;`
    [ ] `require(connectToDatabase);`
    [ ] `return connectToDatabase;`
    ```

4. ES modules are the modern standard, so why be able to read CommonJS?

    ```{mcq}
    Where will you actually run into CommonJS?
    correct: Correct! Node was CommonJS-only for years, so servers, tool configuration files such as `webpack.config.js`, and a great many published packages are written this way. You will read and edit files like these in this course.
    wrong: Think about the files you are about to write in Modules 2 and 3, and what a Webpack configuration file looks like.
    [x] Node servers, tool configuration files, and many published packages still use it
    [ ] It is the only way to load CSS into a page
    [ ] Browsers require it in order to handle click events
    [ ] ES modules cannot export functions, only values
    ```

